<p>Most people who don’t reply aren’t saying no. They’re busy. Following up is a service, as long as each message is short, polite and worth reading.</p>

<h2>A rhythm that works</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Day 0</strong>: call, then text or email if you miss them.</li>
  <li><strong>Day 1</strong>: a second call at a different time of day.</li>
  <li><strong>Day 3</strong>: the <em>Checking in</em> email.</li>
  <li><strong>Day 7</strong>: something useful: an answer to a question they’ll have, an example, a tip.</li>
  <li><strong>Day 14</strong>: the <em>Last try</em> email. It often gets the most replies.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each one is a task on the lead, so it shows in Needs you when it’s due. A playbook can plan them for every new lead, and send the later emails for you.</p>

<h2>Make every follow-up earn its place</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Never just “bumping this up”. Add something: a question, a useful fact, a time to talk.</li>
  <li>Switch channels: some people never answer the phone and reply to texts in seconds.</li>
  <li>Keep it short enough to read on a phone in ten seconds.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When to stop</h2>
<p>After the last try, stop. Mark the lead <em>Lost</em> (you can always move it back), and respect anyone who asks you to stop: that’s what the unsubscribe link is for.</p>
